# swap_terminal environment. COPY THIS TO `.env` AND FILL IN THE FIVE REQUIRED LINES.
#
#     cp .env.example .env     then edit the REQUIRED block below
#
# =============================================================================
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, AND IT IS THE OPERATOR'S OWN WORDS, 2026-10-10
# =============================================================================
#
#   "hey what the fuck? yeah, new shell should not rat fuck the entire fucking
#    machine. it should persist. what the fuck"
#
# They opened a fresh terminal and `docker compose up` refused:
#
#     error while interpolating services.web.volumes.[]: required variable
#     SWAP_DB_DIR is missing a value
#
# MEASURED: the compose files read 91 environment variables, and until this file
# existed every one of them came from a shell export. Five of the 91 have no
# default, so the stack could not start at all in a shell that had not been armed
# by hand -- and the other 86 silently fell back to defaults, which is worse: the
# stack came UP with no RPC credentials, built no chain adapters, and the swap page
# reported chains as NOT CONFIGURED. Nothing said the reason was a new terminal.
#
# DOCKER COMPOSE ALREADY SOLVES THIS and this repository was not using it. Compose
# reads `.env` from the project directory automatically, before interpolating
# anything -- including COMPOSE_FILE, which is what makes a bare `docker compose up`
# pick up the armed overlays. `.gitignore` blocks `.env` and `.env.*` (lines 4, 23
# and 24) and nothing matching them is tracked, which was checked before this file
# was written.
#
# A SHELL EXPORT STILL WINS over a value here, which is the precedence compose
# documents and the one that makes this safe to adopt: an operator who exports
# SWAP_DB_DIR for one command is not fighting this file.
#
# =============================================================================
# SECRETS ARE IN HERE. THAT IS A DECISION, NOT AN ACCIDENT.
# =============================================================================
#
# The lines marked SECRET hold wallet RPC passwords, a Gridcoin wallet passphrase
# and an XRP payout seed. In `.env` they are on disk, unencrypted, readable by
# anything running as this user. The alternative -- retyping them into every new
# terminal -- is what produced the morning this file was written, and CLAUDE.md
# records what the retyping habit already cost: a mangled paste armed the container
# with a fragment of a command instead of the passphrase, and the payout failed
# with `rpc code -14` AFTER a customer's deposit was confirmed.
#
# `chmod 600 .env` after creating it. And note what CLAUDE.md rule 2 records: a
# `.env.bak.2026-03-28_185451` is how a live GRIDCOIN_RPC_PASSWORD reached GitHub.
# Do not copy this file to a name ending in .bak -- `.gitignore` blocks those too
# now, but the habit is what leaked it.
#
# =============================================================================
# THIS FILE IS GENERATED FROM THE COMPOSE FILES, NOT MAINTAINED BY HAND.
# =============================================================================
#
# Every name below is a `${...}` reference in a docker-compose*.yml, and the value
# shown after `=` is the default that file already carries. A variable added to a
# compose file and missing here fails
# tests/test_env_example_covers_every_compose_variable.py, which derives the set the
# same way rather than comparing against a list somebody remembered to update.

# --- COMPOSE ITSELF, and this line is the whole reason a bare `up` can be armed --
#
# COMPOSE_FILE IS NOT A ${...} REFERENCE -- docker compose reads it ITSELF, out of
# this file, before it interpolates anything. That is what lets `docker compose up`
# with no -f flags pick up the armed overlays. Measured on 2026-10-10: without it the
# operator came up UNARMED three times in a row, because arming meant remembering two
# -f flags on every invocation, and a GRC payout then failed with
# "GRIDCOIN_WALLET_PASSPHRASE is not set in this process's environment" after a
# customer's deposit had already confirmed.
#
# Colon-separated, first file wins for scalars, later files override. The default set
# (docker-compose.yml and the two it `include:`s) is what you get with this unset.
#
COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.web.armed-grc.yml
#
# Other overlays, to append with a colon as you arm each chain:
#   docker-compose.web.armed-sol.yml    needs SOL_PAYOUT_KEYPAIR_HOST_FILE
#   docker-compose.web.armed-xrp.yml    needs XRP_PAYOUT_SECRET_SEED
#   docker-compose.grc-desk.yml         needs GRC_DESK_DATADIR
#   docker-compose.web.hostnet.yml      host networking instead of the bridge

# --- REQUIRED FOR A BARE `docker compose up` ------------------------------------
#
# 1 of the 91. Compose refuses to interpolate without these, so the stack does not
# start at all -- which is the loud failure and the easy one.
#
# IT SHIPPED EMPTY AND THAT MADE THIS FILE UNUSABLE, found by the operator on
# 2026-10-10 minutes after pulling it:
#
#     error while interpolating services.web.volumes.[]: required variable
#     SWAP_DB_DIR is missing a value: SWAP_DB_DIR is not set. [...1200 more
#     characters of wall...]
#
# `${SWAP_DB_DIR:?}` refuses UNSET AND EMPTY ALIKE, which the paragraph forty lines
# below this one says in so many words -- "an empty value is refused the same as an
# unset one, so uncommenting without filling in is worse than leaving it alone" --
# and then this line did exactly that. The file stated the rule it broke. A template
# whose whole purpose is `cp .env.example .env` and which cannot start the stack
# after that copy is not a template, it is a trap.
#
# A RELATIVE PATH, resolved by compose against the project directory, which is this
# repository. That makes the default CORRECT rather than merely non-empty: the
# authority is <repo>/swap_terminal/swap_terminal.db and has been since config.py:8,
# so ./swap_terminal is where it already is on a fresh clone. An absolute path would
# have had to be somebody's home directory, which is why the line was left empty in
# the first place -- and empty was the wrong answer to that.
#
# POINT IT SOMEWHERE ELSE IF YOUR DATABASE LIVES ELSEWHERE, and read the two
# warnings in docker-compose.web.yml before you do: it must be the DIRECTORY and not
# the file (WAL keeps -wal and -shm sidecars beside it and they must be one mount),
# and the filesystem must be ext4/xfs/btrfs/zfs on a native Linux host -- on fuseblk,
# virtiofs, nfs, smb2 or 9p, WAL fails by CORRUPTING rather than by erroring.
#
# AND NOT INSIDE A CLOUD-SYNC FOLDER. MEGA, Dropbox, Google Drive, OneDrive,
# Nextcloud and Syncthing all copy the .db and the -wal at different instants, which
# is not a backup but two halves of two different databases; worse, they sync BOTH
# WAYS, so a conflicted copy or a version restore writes a STALE database over the
# authority and every swap that completed since reads as unpaid to a payout worker.
# Operator, 2026-10-10: "we need to have my megashare quit syncing the swap terminal
# db. it makes it sync ever 5 seconds."
#
SWAP_DB_DIR=./swap_terminal

# --- REQUIRED ONLY BY AN OVERLAY YOU PUT IN COMPOSE_FILE -------------------------
#
# These are NOT needed to start. Each is demanded by one overlay above, and only
# when that overlay is in COMPOSE_FILE. Left commented so an unarmed chain does not
# look like a missing setting -- an empty value is refused the same as an unset one,
# so uncommenting without filling in is worse than leaving it alone.
#
# demanded by docker-compose.grc-desk.yml
# GRC_DESK_DATADIR=
# SECRET -- demanded by docker-compose.web.armed-grc.yml
# GRIDCOIN_WALLET_PASSPHRASE=
# SECRET -- demanded by docker-compose.web.armed-sol.yml
# SOL_PAYOUT_KEYPAIR_HOST_FILE=
# SECRET -- demanded by docker-compose.web.armed-xrp.yml
# XRP_PAYOUT_SECRET_SEED=

